  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {d}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {d}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D.$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & {d}_{1}+{d}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+({d}_{1}+{d}_{2}) & = \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1})+({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1}) & = \\\\ 0+0 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ also belongs to $D.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\in D$ and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ \\alpha c & \\alpha d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and so\nthe sum of the entries of $\\alpha A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a+\\alpha b+\\alpha c+\\alpha d & =\\alpha (a+b+c+d) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $D$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"",
